23:15:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s a great question—you’re right that in the physical world, most of the color we see comes from light reflecting off objects. A red apple looks red because it absorbs most colors of light and reflects back mainly red wavelengths into our eyes. That’s called **subtractive color mixing** (used in painting and printing). A computer or TV screen works differently. It doesn’t reflect light—it **creates its own light**. This is called **additive color mixing**, and it’s why screens can glow in a dark room. Here’s how it works step by step: --- ### 1. The screen is made of tiny light sources (pixels) A display is covered in millions of tiny dots called **pixels**. Each pixel is actually three even tinier sub‑pixels: one **red**, one **green**, and one **blue** (RGB). These are tiny lights—usually LEDs in modern screens, or liquid crystal shutters with a backlight in LCDs. ### 2. Additive color mixing Instead of starting with white light and subtracting colors like paint, the screen starts with **black** (no light) and *adds* colored light from the sub-pixels. - If only the red sub-pixel lights up, you see **red**. - If only green lights up, you see **green**. - If red and green light up together at full brightness, your eye mixes them into **yellow**. - Red + blue = **magenta**. - Green + blue = **cyan**. - All three together (red + green + blue) at full brightness make **white**. ### 3. Controlling brightness to get millions of colors Each sub-pixel doesn’t just turn on or off—it can shine at many different intensities (usually 256 levels per color, from 0 = off to 255 = full brightness). By varying how much red, green, and blue light each pixel emits, you can create a huge range of colors. For example: > Red 255, Green 0, Blue 0 → pure red > Red 255, Green 165, Blue 0 → orange > Red 128, Green 0, Blue 128 → a dark purple At normal viewing distance, the three sub-pixels are too small for your eye to see separately, so their light blends into one perceived color for that spot on the screen. --- ### Why it’s different from printed color In print (magazines, posters), you use **ink on paper**. White light from the environment hits the ink, some colors are absorbed, and the rest reflect back to your eye. Printers typically use cyan, magenta, yellow, and black inks (CMYK) because that subtractive mixing works better for reflecting light. That’s why screens (RGB) and printers (CMYK) use different primary color sets—they’re opposite processes. So, in short: - **Objects** → Reflect light (subtractive color) - **Screens** → Emit light (additive color) Your instinct that color is normally about reflection is perfectly correct—screens just cheat by being their own light source, mixing tiny red, green, and blue lights to fool your eyes into seeing every color of the rainbow.
